          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Who does guidance describe as a priority group for influenza vaccination?",
          "answer": "Current guidance on influenza vaccination describes that routine annual vaccination is recommended for everyone aged 6 months and older, with no specific restrictions to high-risk groups. This inclusive approach aims to ensure broad protection against influenza, particularly before the flu season, typically aiming for vaccination before the end of October. This information is derived from the \"Influenza: annual vaccination and the timing of antiviral treatment\" guidance by the National Immunization Advisory Committee and Infectious Diseases Society (source: guideline-influenza-vaccination-and-antivirals).\n\nIn terms of specific high-risk groups, annual influenza vaccination is also emphasized as important for individuals with underlying conditions, such as those with coronary artery disease or chronic respiratory conditions, where vaccination is viewed as a vital cardiovascular measure (source: guideline-coronary-artery-disease-secondary-prevention).\n\nThis guidance indicates that while vaccination is universally recommended, individuals with certain health conditions may particularly benefit from receiving the vaccine each year.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
